{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/planning/evidence/P09/runs/hash-inputs.sh docs/planning/dependencies.md'","aggregated_output":"#!/bin/bash\n# Deterministic inventory of the inputs P09's checks compile, test or build:\n# the Rust crates and host, the desktop app sources and configuration, the\n# contracts package sources and fixtures, the Academy shared source actually\n# consumed (.work/academy/src and its installed packages, staged from\n# .inputs/academy), the native acceptance tools, the lockfiles, the workspace\n# and toolchain manifests, the harness pin and every docs/architecture/*.json.\n# Generated Python bytecode caches are excluded; the captured pending-tree fixture\n# consumed by the L19 driver regression is explicitly included.\n# Every listed path must exist: a missing one fails the inventory (exit 2)\n# instead of producing a partial digest.\n# Usage: hash-inputs.sh <repo-root> <inventory-out>; prints the digest.\nset -euo pipefail\ncd \"$1\"\nDIRS=(crates apps/desktop/src apps/desktop/src-tauri packages/desktop-contracts/src packages/desktop-contracts/fixtures tools/native-acceptance tools/parity tools/academy .work/academy/src .work/academy/packages .inputs/academy)\nFILES=(docs/planning/evidence/C29/live/L19/attempt-01/wizard-verify.json Cargo.toml Cargo.lock rust-toolchain.toml pnpm-lock.yaml package.json pnpm-workspace.yaml resources/harness/pin.json apps/desktop/package.json apps/desktop/index.html packages/desktop-contracts/package.json packages/desktop-contracts/tsconfig.json)\nfor p in \"${DIRS[@]}\"; do [ -d \"$p\" ] || { echo \"inventory: missing directory $p\" >&2; exit 2; }; done\nfor p in \"${FILES[@]}\"; do [ -f \"$p\" ] || { echo \"inventory: missing file $p\" >&2; exit 2; }; done\n{\n  find \"${DIRS[@]}\" -type f -not -path '*/__pycache__/*' -not -path '*/target/*' -not -path '*/node_modules/*' -not -path '*/gen/schemas/*' -not -path '*/.git/*' -print0 | sort -z | xargs -0 shasum -a 256\n  shasum -a 256 \"${FILES[@]}\" apps/desktop/*.ts apps/desktop/*.json docs/architecture/*.json\n} | LC_ALL=C sort -k2 > \"$2\"\n[ -s \"$2\" ] || { echo \"inventory: empty\" >&2; exit 2; }\nshasum -a 256 \"$2\" | cut -d' ' -f1\n# Dependency and source gates\n\nArchitecture proposed exact first-slice versions in docs/architecture/qualification.md.\nAs of C01 (2026-10-17) the desktop graph is resolved, locked and compiled on\nthe macOS ARM64 host; the resolved inputs are recorded below. Later SDK\nfamilies and other inputs remain gated at their consuming chunks.\n\n## Source ownership\n\n| Input          | Inspected revision                       | Ownership/use                                           |\n|----------------|------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|\n| cdot-framework | e3553b27a3d6bf457a2e585c5ae479c7db89a17b | Selective patterns/modules after provenance evaluation  |\n| Desktop v1     | 74754c02b19d87187b6dca81f64550a669b0f841 | Behavioral/contracts reference; preserve checkout       |\n| Academy        | 7f3a28c4082a5bb278bbff5d79fab106c240155a | Shared React source; hosted learning authority          |\n| Native Harness | 9331a5839bc6a763d94dee7584746f58bbbe1181 | Desktop-capable native engine/protocol input            |\n| SDK Phase 3    | ec6230d2a19b9c835c9e4c9487795b38ad538af3 | Accepted scan subset; native/live qualification pending |\n| scm-sdk crate  | crates.io 0.1.0, sha256 6d555d955c3734dd5ee78f7173569bdecd4426095b2ffedc4b77d8f0f2c2e8d9, from 4f65dc48686a3b701ed0585812fcca46859f7e5a | D027 scan input from C02 (scan feature only); 164 package unit tests pass natively; live qualification is C25 |\n| SDK Phase 4    | 32cbcb6d9b04478aeca21e767ed6ddb8949ec7bb | Accepted management/DLP/IAM subset; qualify exact uses  |\n\nDo not consume an active neighboring worktree via an unpinned path and later\nclaim the accepted commit was built. Obtain a clean revision-specific source\nsnapshot/package without secrets or ignored local configuration. Manifest both\nits origin revision and actual source/package hash. A dependency update reopens\nthe affected contract/native/artifact checks and review. Never fabricate SDK\nmethod parity from a similar TypeScript method name.\n\n## C01 resolved inputs\n\nRecorded by C01 and bound to its review. Locks: `Cargo.lock`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`,\n`rust-toolchain.toml`, `docs/architecture/toolchain.json`,\n`docs/architecture/inputs.lock.json`; the controller's dependency fingerprint\nhashes them with the consumed `.inputs/` bytes and tool identities.\n\n| Input | Resolved | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rust toolchain | 1.99.0 (b940084d7 2026-09-28) | Corrected from 1.90.0; proc-macro dylib dyld failure at deployment target 13.0 on macOS 27 (evidence/C01/toolchain-correction.json). Release `strip` removed for the same failure. |\n| Tauri / tauri-build / Tauri CLI / @tauri-apps/api | 2.12.4 / 2.7.3 / 2.12.3 / 2.12.4 | `custom-protocol` added by the Tauri CLI for bundles |\n| ts-rs | 12.0.1 (serde-compat, no-serde-warnings) | Generated declarations committed; `xtask bindings --check` |\n| React / ReactDOM | 19.2.3 | Single instance per consumer (pnpm strict peers) |\n| Tailwind / Vite / Vitest / plugin-react / TypeScript | 4.3.2 / 7.3.6 / 3.2.6 / 5.2.0 / 5.9.3 | Matched to Academy's resolved lock instead of the earlier 4.1.18 / 7.3.0 proposal |\n| Build Node / pnpm | 24.21.0 / 10.6.5 (corepack shim in `.tools/bin`) | Build-only; never shipped |\n| Academy shared presentation | `ai-security-academy` 891f65eef76b53eb87155d0f0f5622228e217a2d on branch `v2/desktop-presentation` (base 7f3a28c4) | Pushed to the Academy origin on 2026-10-17 (owner-approved; main untouched). `xtask fetch-inputs --source <academy clone>` materializes `.inputs/academy` (tree sha256 1c4ce702…); a fresh clone from origin reproduces it (evidence/C01/clean-checkout). |\n\nThird-party notices for the shipped graph are in `docs/architecture/notice-inventory.json`\n(`xtask notices --check`). The Academy package is Academy-owned and recorded by\nits pinned commit, not relicensed.\n\n## Required gates\n\n- **Distribution terms:** G01 records owner-approved v2 source/binary terms,\n  Academy redistribution permission and every framework-derived import's\n  disposition under [distribution.md](distribution.md). No extraction before\n  clearance; C26 verifies notices/terms against exact shipped inventory.\n\n- **Provenance:** evaluate each direct import's actual license/notice and allowed\n  usage. cdot-framework is not blanket MIT and its billing-retention condition\n  cannot be erased by renaming. Prefer independently implemented patterns where\n  direct copying is unsuitable. Academy sharing is owner-directed; establish\n  package ownership and third-party notices rather than inventing a license.\n- **Academy packaging:** React19/Tailwind4/Vite7 are observed in Academy, while\n  the framework uses React18/Tailwind3. Select a compatible pinned desktop stack\n  and one shared presentation source. The existing UI package re-exports app\n  source and is not a ready independently reusable full learner app.\n- **Shared-source changes:** propose the smallest extraction that supports actual\n  web/desktop consumers. Keep Academy web behavior and hosted contracts stable;\n  run both projects' affected tests. Pin the reviewed shared revision/package\n  before completing its consuming desktop chunk.\n- **SDK scan:** ec6230d2's accepted subset supplies synchronous scan. It requires\n  explicit scan credentials; management OAuth is separate. Accepted Linux\n  reports do not establish macOS ARM64 support. Qualify the native compiler/TLS\n  dependencies, runtime integration, scanner contracts, cancellation, sanitized\n  errors, and unknown outcomes in C02–C03.\n- **SDK administration:** 32cbcb6d's accepted Phase4 is a candidate input for\n  C08–C12. Map every actual operation, option, pagination bound, and error to\n  accepted source/tests. SDK family labels are insufficient proof of parity.\n- **SDK later families:** Phase5 Red Team/Model and Phase6 Gateway were developing\n  or pending during inspection. Their exact accepted pins are prerequisites to\n  C13/C16–C18. Do not consume unfinished work or substitute mocks as completion.\n  Stable v2 must qualify the SDK subset it actually uses, including native/live\n  evidence; it need not claim every unrelated SDK method is complete.\n- **Harness:** retain the desktop-capable native engine/protocol after source and\n  package checks. Do not substitute an older canonical checkout or npm launcher.\n  Rebuild/reseal v2 resources to omit Node/managed CLI where proven unnecessary;\n  old v1 resource hashes do not attest the changed v2 bundle. Preserve inference\n  and MCP routing/context/tool restrictions.\n- **Native host:** select pinned Tauri2/Rust/toolchain dependencies compatible\n  with macOS ARM64 and the chosen SDK. Resolve one application-owned async\n  runtime strategy; do not create an independent runtime per request.\n- **Identity:** specify independent v2 bundle/display/data/Keychain identities in\n  G01. Test v1/v2 coexistence, reset, uninstall, import, and rollback. Do not\n  silently reuse a v1 namespace or change the learner's remote account.\n- **Live environment:** identify designated test tenants/accounts, scopes,\n  reversible resources, budgets, and cleanup/readback before C25 execution.\n  Prod configuration discovery is not mutation authorization. Missing access is\n  a real dependency gap; never report skipped tests as passed.\n- **Hosted OAuth registrations:** G01 maps reuse/new-client/redirect/scope needs\n  for Academy device authorization, Harness company SSO and MCP. Responsible\n  Academy/Gateway/IdP operators own authorized staging/configuration changes and\n  deployment. V2 identity alone neither proves a new registration is required\n  nor authorizes production changes. Resolve before the consuming live tests.\n- **Signing:** identify an authorized Developer ID, provisioning/entitlement\n  requirements, notarization access, and a native acceptance host. Access secrets\n  only at use time through approved storage, never committed plan variables.\n\n## Official host references\n\nTauri supports Rust commands and async messages between presentation and host;\nthe selected command/module interface still requires G01 design and review.\n[Calling Rust](https://v2.tauri.app/develop/calling-rust/)\n\nWindow/WebView capability grants require deliberate scoping; renderer grants do\nnot replace Rust's application authority checks.\n[Capabilities](https://v2.tauri.app/security/capabilities/)\n\nDMG distribution and macOS signing are separate from successful compilation.\n[DMG packaging](https://v2.tauri.app/distribute/dmg/),\n[macOS signing/notarization](https://v2.tauri.app/distribute/sign/macos/).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1364,1398p' docs/planning/decisions.md; sed -n '1711,1838p' docs/planning/decisions.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1364,1398p' docs/planning/decisions.md; sed -n '1711,1838p' docs/planning/decisions.md\"","aggregated_output":"## D046 — The native Harness engine: sealed resource, lifecycle and the C19 conversation\n\nDecided in C19 (implementer decision for the C19 milestones, recorded for\nreview).\n\n**The engine and its seal.** The engine is the pinned build of\n`prisma-airs-harness` 9331a58 (protocol `airs-desktop/1`, runtime\n0.1.7-desktop.2). It is not rebuilt here. It is the binary v1 sealed at v1\ncommit 74754c02: `packaging/harness/darwin-arm64.tar.gz`, a Git LFS object\nwith sha256 e79f564a…. That binary's SHA-256 (f8e49cee…) matches its\n`BUILD-INFO.json`, which names the source commit 9331a58 and the build\ncommand, and v1's runtime pin.\n\n`tools/harness/seal.py` reseals it for v2 and takes only the engine and\nits notices:\n- `airs-harness`;\n- `BUILD-INFO.json`, `DEPENDENCIES.json`, `LICENSE` and `NOTICE`;\n- `licenses/engine/`, the engine's dependencies' licenses.\n\nNode, the launcher scripts, the managed CLI and its `node_modules` are\nleft out. The output is deterministic. It consists of:\n- `resources/harness/darwin-arm64.tar.gz` (Git LFS, 113 MB): the files\n  above and a `MANIFEST.json` inventory;\n- `resources/harness/pin.json`: the pin record, compiled into\n  desktop-core.\n\n`xtask native-prepare` extracts the archive and checks every file against\nthe inventory and the pin. A Tauri overlay of its own,\n`tauri.harness.conf.json`, bundles it as `Contents/Resources/harness/`; the\nfixture overlay still only renames the identity. Signed release packaging\nis C26.\n\n**Verification on every start.** Before a conversation reads its key or\nlaunches anything, the bundled executable must be:\n- an absolute path named `airs-harness`;\n## D048 — Harness desktop tools, project folder, approvals and proposals\n\nDecided in C21 (implementer decision for the C21 milestones, recorded for\nreview).\n\n**Desktop tools.** The engine is given one dynamic-tool namespace,\n`airs_desktop`, and nothing else of the desktop. Its tools are typed:\n- `inventory`, `detail`: the Runtime Security inventories C08 reads.\n  The DLP catalog is not offered.\n- `session`, `search`: C11's investigation reads. Session content is\n  already stripped by the management layer.\n- `redTeam`, `supplyChain`: C13's observation reads.\n- `scan`: one C03 scan with a profile named by the model, through the\n  tenant's own scan key.\n- `cases`: the C12 library's names and expectations.\n- `proposeChange`: stages a change for the operator to review (below).\n\nEvery call goes through the same admission as the operator's own reads:\n`admit_management`, `admit_scan` and the library. The call runs under the\nconversation's context and is refused once that context is stale. The\ndesktop holds the credentials. Results are JSON:\n- JSON carried as text is opened first: at most 4 layers deep and 4 MiB.\n  Text still encoded past those bounds is replaced by a note, never\n  passed on unchecked (P06, C21 reviews F2 and R2-F2);\n- field names that carry credentials are removed at any depth (14\n  names, case- and dash-insensitive);\n- the conversation's key is redacted;\n- a longer result becomes `{\"cut\": note, \"partial\": text}`, shortened\n  until the whole envelope, escaping included, fits 24 KiB (P06, C21\n  review R2-F4).\n\nA call is parsed into a closed set:\n- `deny_unknown_fields`;\n- arguments of at most 40 KiB, pages of at most 50 and scan text of at\n  most 16 KiB;\n- any credential-like argument name is refused.\n\nThe desktop runs a call only when it belongs to the turn being answered,\nand only once per call ID; a repeat is a protocol failure. Anything else\nis answered \"This tool or its arguments are unavailable.\" and nothing\nruns. At most 8 calls or approvals wait at a time. A call has 60 s, and\nthe engine's pending tool calls are dropped with the turn (interrupt or\nend). Each call carries its run and turn lease. When the turn ends, the\ncall is cancelled, and so is the runtime operation it admitted (P06, C21\nreviews F1 and R2-F1): queued work settles NotSent and sends nothing;\nwork already dispatched keeps its own outcome; no result is sent late. This follows C19's rule that a reply to a request the engine forgot\nis fatal.\n\n**Proposals, never Apply.** `proposeChange` accepts Runtime and DLP kinds\nonly, with create, update or delete. Gateway changes need the operator's\nown workspace review (C17). Before staging, the proposal passes the same\nstatic checks a review makes, through the tenant's management client\n(`changes::normalize`). Nothing is read or sent at that point.\n- A conversation holds at most 8 proposals, numbered `P-1`… The model is\n  told that nothing was applied.\n- Review opens the proposal with the operator's existing\n  `review_change`. It is refused (`Busy`) while any change review is\n  open, so the operator's own draft or review is never replaced.\n- Administration shows the opened review with the proposal's exact\n  draft, so Apply sends what was reviewed. Only the operator's\n  `RequestApply` and the native confirmation (C09) write.\n- A proposal reads `inReview` only while its review is the open one, then\n  `closed`. Dismissed proposals are hidden.\n\n**Project folder and access.** The operator chooses one folder per tenant\nin the native \"Choose project folder\" sheet. The folder is stored as\nnonsecret state with a write flag that is off by default. These folders\nare refused:\n- the home folder itself, and `.ssh`, `.aws`, `.gnupg`, `.config`, `.kube`,\n  `.docker`, `Library`, `.azure` and `.netrc` under it;\n- `/System`, `/usr`, `/bin`, `/sbin`, `/private/etc`, `/private/var/db`,\n  `/private/var/root`, `/Library`, `/Applications`, `/dev` and `/cores`;\n- this app's data root, and any folder containing it.\n\nThe folder and its access are part of the engine binding:\n- The bootstrap carries `projectPath` and `localAccess`\n  (`readOnly`/`workspaceWrite`).\n- The thread carries `cwd` and the `read-only` or `workspace-write`\n  sandbox.\n- Without a folder, the thread has no local environment.\n\nChanging the folder or its access retires the running conversation. A\nsaved conversation whose folder or access differs reads `otherProject` and\ncannot be continued: a new conversation is needed. Restoring a compatible\none restores no approval, and no command is run again.\n\n**Approvals.** The engine's command and file-change requests wait for the\noperator under the desktop's own token, 12 random bytes. A token is valid\nonly for that run and turn and is cleared at interrupt and at turn end, so\na stale or repeated decision is refused. A request is declined at once,\nwithout asking, when any of these holds:\n- there is no folder;\n- it is a file change while changes are off;\n- it asks for added permissions, network access or another root;\n- it belongs to another turn;\n- what it would do cannot be shown whole: a command longer than 8 KiB (or\n  none), or a file change whose every file is not named whole (none\n  named, a change without a path, more than 64 files or a path past\n  1 KiB) (P06, C21 reviews F3 and R2-F3: never shown cut or partial);\n- 8 decisions are already waiting.\n\nThe pinned engine's own desktop sandbox enforces the rest. The negative\nprobes are recorded:\n- one process, with exec replacement only, so children and pipelines fail\n  with \"fork: Operation not permitted\";\n- no network;\n- reads outside the folder fail with \"Operation not permitted\";\n- writes only inside the folder, and only with `workspaceWrite`.\n\nThe engine's own patch writer (`apply_patch`) names its files only with\nthe change item, which may reach the desktop before or after the approval\nrequest. Asked first, the request is declined at once; asked after, the\napproval shows the named files whole. Approved, the patch still fails to\nwrite in this build: it runs in the engine's narrower helper sandbox (the\nengine is not changed). Allowed shell commands are what change the folder.\nBoth orders are exercised (harness_tools, native C21-N04).\n\n**Renewal with changes allowed.** When a company sign-in renewal\n(D047) would restart an engine that may change the folder, the message is\nheld (`confirmWrite`). Allow renews, restarts and restores with the same\naccess, then sends. Not now gives the message back, and nothing is\nrestarted, restored or sent.\n\n**Out of scope here:** MCP servers and their token rotation (C22);\nimported history (C23); live management, Gateway and model qualification\n(C25).\n\n## D049 — Workspace MCP servers for the Harness: catalog, desktop OAuth, grants and approval\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
